        fp: "A manufacturer of high-precision industrial robotics entered into a written contract with an automotive assembly plant to supply 20 robotic welding arms for $600,000, payable upon delivery scheduled for November 1[cite: 43]. On September 10, the manufacturer sent a signed letter to the assembly plant stating: 'Due to severe microchip supply constraints, we will be unable to deliver the robotic arms until February 1 of next year; we consider our current contract canceled.'[cite: 43] On September 12, the assembly plant phoned two alternative automated equipment manufacturers to inquire about market prices for equivalent robotic welding arms, learning that replacement units would cost $750,000[cite: 43]. However, the assembly plant neither executed a replacement purchase agreement nor notified the original manufacturer that it considered the repudiation final[cite: 43]. On September 18, the original manufacturer secured an emergency allocation of microchips and sent an expedited certified letter to the assembly plant: 'We have resolved our supply bottleneck and hereby retract our cancellation; we will deliver all 20 robotic arms on November 1 as originally scheduled.'[cite: 43] The assembly plant received this letter on September 20, but immediately contracted with a third-party supplier for $750,000 and sued the original manufacturer for $150,000 in cover damages[cite: 43].",
