        exp: "Rule: Under the standard expectation damages formula for a service or construction provider following a repudiation by the buyer/owner before completion (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347), the non-breaching provider is entitled to recover: (Costs Incurred) + (Unrealized Net Profit) - (Any Payments Received to Date)[cite: 43]. Here, the architectural firm incurred $45,000 in direct labor and consultant expenditures and lost its bargained-for net profit of $30,000 ($45,000 + $30,000 = $75,000 total claim)[cite: 43]. Subtracting the $30,000 advance deposit already received yields a net expectation damages award of $45,000 (Option A)[cite: 43]. Option B is incorrect because awarding the full unpaid balance of $90,000 ignores the firm's duty to mitigate by ceasing performance, improperly granting costs the firm never had to expend[cite: 43]. Option C is incorrect because awarding only lost profit fails to reimburse the firm for uncompensated reliance expenditures already incurred[cite: 43]. Option D is incorrect because it awards only net reliance costs while completely omitting the firm's rightful lost expectation profit[cite: 43]."
