        fp: "An equipment rental company leased a commercial skid-steer loader to an excavation contractor for a one-week job[cite: 43]. On the fourth day, without notice to or authorization from the rental company, the contractor transported the skid-steer to a remote mountain quarry to perform heavy rock-breaking operations that exceeded the machine's rated duty cycle[cite: 43]. While fracturing granite boulders, a hydraulic cylinder fractured and the chassis cracked, diminishing the loader's fair market value from $45,000 to $15,000[cite: 43]. Realizing the extent of the damage, the contractor had the loader towed back to the rental yard and offered to return it along with payment for the full week's rental fee plus $5,000 in repair estimates[cite: 43]. The rental company refused tender of the damaged loader and immediately filed a tort action for conversion, demanding the pre-injury fair market value of $45,000[cite: 43].",
