        fp: "Defendant wanted to break into an occupied, single-family residential home at night to steal valuable vintage musical instruments[cite: 43]. At 2:00 AM, Defendant approached the home wearing a dark ski mask and rubber gloves[cite: 43]. Defendant walked to a ground-floor sunroom and found a set of French doors[cite: 43]. The doors were closed and secured by an exterior slide bolt latch mounted at waist height[cite: 43]. Defendant slid the metal bolt back with his fingers, pulled the right door open two feet into the dark sunroom, and reached his arm through the opening holding a flashlight to illuminate the interior floor[cite: 43]. Before Defendant could place his feet or any other part of his body across the threshold, an interior German Shepherd guard dog began barking violently and lunged toward the doorway[cite: 43]. Defendant yanked his arm back outside, slammed the French door shut, and sprinted down the street, where he was detained by a police K-9 unit two blocks away[cite: 43]. The jurisdiction adheres strictly to traditional common law criminal definitions[cite: 43].",
