        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "A homeowner entered into a written contract with an architectural restoration firm to restore an antique, ornamental copper cupola mounted on the roof of his Victorian residence for $18,000, payable upon completion on August 1[cite: 43]. On July 10, after the restoration firm had completed approximately 50% of the copper repoussé detailing and spent $6,000 in specialized materials and master artisan labor, a freak lightning bolt from an unpredicted summer thunderstorm struck the main roof of the residence[cite: 43]. The resulting fire consumed the entire roof, completely destroying the residence and incinerating the cupola[cite: 43]. The restoration firm demanded payment of $9,000 from the homeowner for the value of the work performed prior to the fire[cite: 43]. The homeowner refused to pay, arguing that because the contract was an entire, non-severable agreement that was never completed, the firm was entitled to nothing[cite: 43].",
